Roots trusted in addition to sfw's own CA, for the package managers wrapped by
sfw_extra_roots (see etc/zshrc).

sfw intercepts the registries it knows and tunnels every other host untouched,
while handing its child a trust store holding only its own CA -- so a tunnelled
host, such as an internal package index, cannot be verified at all. Each root
below is one whose issuance is trusted enough to reach such an index; adding a
root widens trust to every certificate that root signs, which is why the file
lists roots one at a time rather than restoring the public trust store.

Amazon Root CA 1 -- the internal Python index is served from S3, whose
certificate chains to this root. Anchoring at the root rather than at the
issuing intermediate survives Amazon's intermediate rotation.

